1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:02,880
Your AI second brain
remembers everything.

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:04,180
Do you?

3
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:06,395
You ask your AI something hard,

4
00:00:06,464 --> 00:00:08,560
and the answer is crystal clear.

5
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:09,850
It all makes sense.

6
00:00:09,850 --> 00:00:11,552
You feel like you've learned it.

7
00:00:12,212 --> 00:00:15,342
But a week later, you ask
the exact same question.

8
00:00:16,002 --> 00:00:19,322
Memory fades fast at
first, then slowly.

9
00:00:19,332 --> 00:00:22,249
Researchers measured
that curve in 1885,

10
00:00:22,249 --> 00:00:25,692
and a 2015 replication
found a similar curve.

11
00:00:26,352 --> 00:00:30,372
In a classic experiment,
students studied short passages.

12
00:00:30,372 --> 00:00:32,462
Then they re-read one passage.

13
00:00:32,462 --> 00:00:35,932
For another, they closed it and
wrote down what they remembered.

14
00:00:36,592 --> 00:00:41,922
Five minutes later,
re-reading won: 81% to 75%.

15
00:00:42,582 --> 00:00:45,112
But after a week, it flipped.

16
00:00:46,072 --> 00:00:50,532
Recalling beat
re-reading: 56% to 42%.

17
00:00:50,892 --> 00:00:54,966
In a second experiment, re-readers
felt surest they'd remember,

18
00:00:55,047 --> 00:00:56,702
yet remembered less.

19
00:00:57,462 --> 00:01:00,266
So after your AI explains
something, close the answer,

20
00:01:00,266 --> 00:01:01,615
say it from memory,

21
00:01:01,768 --> 00:01:04,192
then come back at gaps
that keep growing.

22
00:01:04,302 --> 00:01:08,582
Spaced repetition tools like
FSRS schedule those gaps for you.

23
00:01:09,342 --> 00:01:11,292
Reading feels like learning.

24
00:01:11,292 --> 00:01:15,862
Pulling it from memory, a little
later each time, makes it stick.

